{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the S6 repair at head 47ab3fa277, tree 93e90eea, for request ddf2a597 and review request cefa3422. Verdict: APPROVED.\n\nThis reviews the diff from 4b9f6e89 and the new gate, against my report 766b6c61. The earlier conclusions there carry over, because the diff doesn't reopen that code. I read the whole source diff and the expiry order in `judge.ts`. I ran no independent second read on this delta, and I did not rerun the controls.\n\n## My seven items\n\n1. The card has an exit. One minute past `notAfter`, Check original sends the same bytes once, and the scope's answer ends the record. The new case shows that a retained card signs nothing, the check sends nothing before `notAfter`, and the scope's refusal then frees the card.\n2. `not-found` now says that this server keeps no record, and that this doesn't show the act wasn't sent. The card's line says the same.\n3. `#refuseWhileOpen` runs before a signature, on both act and View diff dispatch.\n4. `shownPath` caps a path at 1,024 bytes, statements of a bound use a brief form, and `#emit` keeps room for the closing line.\n5. `byteString` spreads bounded slices, so a huge name no longer throws.\n6. Paths escape the backslash, C0, DEL, C1, bidi controls and line separators, and the form is now unambiguous.\n7. `COMMIT_ID` admits only SHA-1, and it's checked before a signature or a spent credential.\n\n## Your points\n\n1. Your route is better than the one I suggested, and I found no harm that the order misses. The answer comes from the scope, so no custody phase changes and no \"not sent\" state can be wrong. `judge.ts:217-224` answers an accepted intent `accepted-before` at any time, under its key, and refuses an unlanded one as `expired` only after that check. A different intent under the key answers `mismatch`. If the scope's clock lags by more than the margin, the act lands, but it's the act its signer asked for, its signed revisions still bind it, and `#refuseWhileOpen` kept this server from signing anything else at that scope in between. The planner accepted a different route in f2ffbcfc, and it asked to hear of anything that differs, which you did in 02d014d9. I saw no planner answer yet, so the planner may still overrule this.\n\n2. It doesn't widen who can resend. The key is the card's server-held key, the signer and record are the same, and `view_act` takes only an ID. The generic settle tool passes no `resendExpired`, so it never resends. Terminal phases and standalone records stay out. Non-application services now go through `#viewSettle` where they used `this.call(\"settle\")`, so those card checks skip `call`'s metrics and tool admission. It's small, and the same retained key was already all they could name. Note it for the hosted deployment, because this path reaches hosted cards.\n\n3. Yes, that's enough for the merge path. A second custody home can still send, and the scope's revisions and merge guards bind that. `prepared` records have sent nothing.\n\n4. Yes. File text has to stay exact for `git apply`, the card sets it as text and not markup, and the head comment in `diff.ts` says to read it as data. A model still sees raw bidi characters in file content, so an agent's guidance should keep treating repository text as data. That's the same omission I raised on the S5 prompt. Zero-width characters in a path also stay unescaped, which is harmless to the line form.\n\n## What holds\n\n- The gate at 3a05555a exits 0. Its `test.log` shows Node 98, Counting 56, Plain 3,217 with one skip and Native 897 with two skips. From there to this head, only the note and the manifest change.\n- The evidence verifies. All 56 manifest rows match, the manifest has digest 40541be9, the directory has 109 files, its `SHA256SUMS` reruns clean with digest ad432fdb, and no file is writable. The earlier sealed directory stays as it was.\n- `git merge-tree --write-tree` onto main 09420198 gives tree 93e90eea. The seals change only for `service.ts`, and `security-entry-points` passes 10 of 10.\n- I ran `diff.test.ts` (6 of 6) and `view-sessions.test.ts` (8 of 8) once each. `check-notes` gives 0 warnings, `check-commit-messages` checks 11 with 0 flagged, and `git diff --check` is clean. The worktree stayed clean.\n\n## Still open, as the note says\n\n- The hosted witness stays owed, and per f2ffbcfc the Change view isn't finished for a reviewer until it runs. View diff may need a design change then.\n- Adversarial `decodePack` cases, CPU time on a 32 MiB pack, the browser's View diff, and a live never-arrived run.\n- The 60-second margin is a choice, because the contract states no clock bound.\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe exit uses custody and the scope as they are, with no new state. Approved.\n\n## Security\n\nThe bad advice is gone, a retained card can't sign during an unknown, and a hostile repository can no longer break the answer's bounds or disguise a path. Resending is limited to the same bytes, under the same key. Approved.\n\n## Simplification\n\nSeveral checks consolidate into `commitIdFault`, `byteString` and `#openOriginal`. Approved.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: approved.\n- Security: approved.\n- Simplification: approved.\n- The request's checks hold, with witness 5 on stand-ins and the hosted witness owed. No deployment follows until J4 closes.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:f2c28586406771405bd01767b538b9a1d8990ec5","binding":"assigned","head":"47ab3fa277a00638d652c285d83c51eb6a88cf88","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:3757c25e505f24694351d39c9f5f0136fc9f03dd\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:ca23fc67fe4892bb9ddcfaef61b23a39ccb4891f\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:3757c25e505f24694351d39c9f5f0136fc9f03dd","review_path":"reviewguard@1","stale":"true","stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a23